      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agreed, and fixed in patch set 3: decodeAwarenessUpdate() now returns d.atEnd(), so a body that does not end exactly where its entry count says it does is rejected rather than silently truncated.\n\nChecked against the real thing before changing it, since a stricter decoder is only safe if genuine frames are exact. An awareness update produced by y-protocols/awareness is [0x01, 0x19, 0x01, 0xb9, 0x60, 0x01, 0x14, ...20 bytes of JSON]: a 25-byte body holding exactly one entry, ending on the last byte of the state string. Compiled against the patched decoder, that frame is still accepted and decodes to clientId 12345 / clock 1, the same frame with one padding byte appended is now refused, and our own encoder round-trips. So the check costs nothing in interoperability.\n\nIt is also safe by construction: the body is delimited by readVarUint8Array() upstream in onAwarenessMessage(), so atEnd() is asking whether the sender filled the length it declared -- not whether more messages follow.",
